import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Can a car battery die without warning?",
    a: "Yes — especially in Dubai. Heat damage builds up inside the battery over the summer, and the battery can then fail suddenly on the first morning it's asked for a little more. That's why a test every year after the second summer is worth doing even if the car starts fine.",
  },
  {
    q: "How long does a car battery last in Dubai?",
    a: 'Usually around 2–3 years for a conventional battery, sometimes less for cars parked outdoors in full sun. AGM batteries tend to last longer. More in <a href="/guides/how-long-car-battery-lasts-uae/">how long a car battery lasts in the UAE</a>.',
  },
  {
    q: "Is a clicking sound always the battery?",
    a: 'Usually, but not always. Rapid clicking normally means the battery is too weak to turn the starter. A single loud click with full dashboard lights can point to the starter motor. See <a href="/guides/car-wont-start-battery-or-alternator/">battery vs alternator vs starter</a>.',
  },
];
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="signs-car-battery-is-dying"
  service="battery_testing"
  lead="Most car batteries give some warning before they fail completely. In Dubai's heat, those warnings can be short — here are the nine signs to watch for, and what to do about each."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    A car battery rarely dies out of nowhere. It loses capacity gradually, and the car starts telling you — sometimes for
    weeks, sometimes only for a few days. In a Dubai summer, when under-bonnet temperatures are extreme, that window can be
    very short. Knowing the signs means you can replace the battery on your schedule, not on the hard shoulder.
  </p>

  <h2>1. The engine cranks slowly</h2>
  <p>
    The most common early warning. Instead of a quick, crisp start, the engine turns over lazily — "rur-rur-rur" — before
    catching. It's often most noticeable first thing in the morning or after the car has been parked for a day or two.
    A slow crank means the battery can no longer deliver the current the starter motor needs.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Clicking when you turn the key or press start</h2>
  <p>
    Rapid clicking with no crank usually means the battery has enough charge to power the dashboard and the starter
    solenoid, but not enough to turn the engine. At this point the battery is effectively flat. A{" "}
    <a href="/jump-start-dubai/">jump start</a> will usually get you going, but the battery should be tested.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Dim headlights or interior lights</h2>
  <p>
    Lights that look weaker than usual — particularly when the engine is off or idling — suggest low battery voltage. If
    the lights brighten noticeably when you rev the engine, the battery is struggling and the alternator is covering for
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Battery or charging warning light</h2>
  <p>
    The battery-shaped warning light usually signals a charging-system problem rather than the battery itself, but either
    way it means the electrical system isn't healthy. Don't ignore it — a failing alternator will flatten even a new
    battery within a day or two.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Stop-start stops working</h2>
  <p>
    On cars with automatic stop-start, the system monitors battery health. When the battery weakens, the car quietly
    disables stop-start to protect its ability to restart. If your stop-start has stopped activating on a hot day with the
    AC on, that can be normal — but if it never activates any more, the battery is a likely cause.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Electrical gremlins</h2>
  <p>
    Radio presets resetting, the clock losing time, windows moving slowly, random warning messages — especially on
    European cars — can all be symptoms of low voltage. Modern cars are very sensitive to it; on a Range Rover or a
    Mercedes, a weak battery can trigger several unrelated-looking warnings at once.
  </p>

  <h2>7. A swollen or leaking battery case</h2>
  <p>
    Heat can make a battery case bulge, and a damaged battery may leak or show white or blue-green corrosion around the
    terminals. A swollen battery should be replaced promptly — don't try to charge or jump it yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>8. A rotten-egg smell</h2>
  <p>
    A sulphur smell around the bonnet can mean the battery is overheating or being overcharged and venting gas. Stop using
    the car and have the battery and charging system checked.
  </p>

  <h2>9. The battery is simply old</h2>
  <p>
    Look for a date code on the battery label. In the UAE, a conventional battery that's past two to three years old is
    living on borrowed time, even if it still starts the car. Heat damage accumulates each summer, and the first hard
    start in a heatwave is often the one it can't manage.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do if you notice these signs</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Get it tested.</strong> A proper test measures the battery's health and the alternator's output — it takes
      a few minutes. Our <a href="/battery-testing-dubai/">battery test</a> is free and done at your car.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Don't wait for the no-start.</strong> A battery showing several of these signs is close to the end. Replacing
      it at home or at the office is far easier than on the roadside.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Replace with the right type.</strong> If your car needs an EFB or AGM battery, fit one — see{" "}
      <a href="/guides/agm-vs-efb-vs-conventional-battery/">AGM vs EFB vs conventional</a>.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If you're already stuck, don't worry: <a href="/car-battery-replacement-dubai/">battery replacement</a> is done at
    your location, 24/7, anywhere in Dubai.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
